      <div class="fade-up about-copy">
        <div class="section-eyebrow">About</div>
        <h2 style="margin-bottom:26px;">From Langley to the World Stage.</h2>
        <p>Hawley Awad grew up on a hobby farm in Langley, British Columbia, riding through the 4-H Horse Club and the local Pony Club — chasing the same dream so many young riders carry: the Olympics, someday.</p>
        <p>That dream took shape in 1994, when a gangly four-year-old off-the-track Thoroughbred named Livingstone came into her life. The partnership clicked immediately, and by 21 — on the strength of a top finish at the North American Young Riders' Championships — Hawley earned a spot on the Canadian Eventing Team. "Hank" carried her to a team silver at the 2003 Pan American Championships and, the following year, to her first Olympic start, representing Canada in Athens.</p>
        <p>A second horse of a lifetime followed in the mare Gin &amp; Juice — five-star completions at Badminton and Burghley, a USEA Mare of the Year title, a World Equestrian Games team silver, and a return Olympic trip to London in 2012. Years later, the mare Jollybo carried Hawley to another World Championship selection, representing Canada individually in Tryon.</p>
        <p>Now based in Temecula, California, Hawley trains and competes upper-level horses while coaching riders of every level — passing on the horsemanship and patience that built a career spanning four World Championships, two Olympic Games, two Pan American Games, and an FEI World Cup Final.</p>

